{
  "image_name": "0a8dd882de72b83faaed09687dc104a2.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how failures of immune tolerance at several developmental stages can produce autoreactive B and T cells and lead to disease. It follows lymphocyte development from bone marrow and thymus into secondary lymphoid organs, highlighting points where self-reactive cells are normally removed or corrected and where defects let them escape.\n\nOn the B-cell side, immature autoreactive B cells should undergo receptor editing or clonal deletion and apoptosis in the bone marrow. The diagram shows that defects in these central checkpoints increase release of autoreactive naïve B cells. For T cells, autoreactive thymic T cells should be deleted or diverted into regulatory T cells, but defects in central T-cell tolerance reduce this control and allow more autoreactive T cells to exit the thymus.\n\nIn peripheral lymphoid organs, activated T and B cells interact through TCR–MHC class II and CD40L–CD40 signals, promoting affinity maturation. If peripheral B-cell tolerance is impaired, autoreactive memory B cells and plasma cells form and produce autoantibodies, especially against interferons. The figure links these outcomes to specific trigger factors such as viral infection, cytokine therapy, vaccination, TLR agonists, and certain genetic or HLA backgrounds, with consequences including life-threatening COVID-19 and adult-onset immunodeficiency.",
